   118	   - **The rule:** both families mark the question `opened`, or `narrower` with both saying it carries the matter's main outcome
   119	     (ACCEPTANCE-DESIGN A; `tally_week.covered`). `broader` and `missed` do not cover.
   120	   - **Blinding:** the readers see each draw's library under a random X/Y label, the key outside the repository, and are told to ignore
   121	     style.
   122	   - **Disagreements:** two third readers, one per family. A remaining split is a split.
   123	3. **Same question:**
   124	   - **A question** is an id in a system-draw's library at the cutoff, the system's own identity. Distinct questions are distinct ids.
   125	   - **Matter to question:** the mapping rule above.
   126	   - **Question to question:** two questions of one library are the same bet when the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move
   127	     apart (A1's rule; D248). Of such a pair, **the earlier-opened question is the original and the later one the duplicate** (as
   128	     `week/brief-A2.md` named the order).
   129	   - **During mapping:** the readers, who read each whole library, also mark its duplicates.
   130	   - **Distinct bets** are the ids less the duplicates both families mark. The 30-question minimum counts distinct bets. A split duplicate is
   131	     counted both ways, and the minimum must hold both ways.
   132	   - **On cards:** a duplicate stays a question in the worth guardrail.
   133	4. **The cards and their marks:**
   134	   - **Worth tracking:** worth, trivia or duplicate. Each card lists its library's other questions in opening order, with their opening days.
   135	     `duplicate` is marked only on the later question of a same-bet pair.
   136	   - **Answer:** supported, unsupported, insufficient, or no answer (a question not judged by the cutoff).
   137	   - **Cannot tell:** a split after the debate rounds, resolved in each counting by item 7.
   138	5. **Served in a draw:** at least one covering question is marked `worth` and supported, in each counting after item 7's resolution. A matter **served by a system** is served in
   139	   at least 2 of its 3 draws.
   140	6. **The quantities:** a = served only by the new system; b = served only by the old; N = all listed matters.
   141	7. **The two countings.** A split ("cannot tell" after the debate rounds) resolves to one of the marks its readers actually gave.
   142	   - **Counting 1:** the new system's cards take the most favourable mark given; the old system's cards take the least favourable.
   143	   - **Counting 2:** the reverse.
   144	   - **The order:** for the answer, supported > insufficient > unsupported; for worth, worth > duplicate or trivia.
   145	   - **How a resolved mark counts:** it counts as that mark. A resolved supported or unsupported mark enters the decided denominators. A
   146	     resolved insufficient mark stays out of them, as any insufficient mark does.
   147	   - A guardrail subgroup is **exercised** only with at least 10 cards decided **without** a split. A subgroup that is not exercised cannot
   148	     breach, and it makes the verdict "no practical winner" (PRD §1.5).
   149	8. **The guardrails** (D428's measure, which D665 turns into these guardrails):
   150	   - **The sample:** the random guardrail sample only, 35 questions per system-draw by seed 20261117, or all of them when a library holds
   151	     fewer. Covering cards are not in these shares. The three draws are pooled per system.
   152	   - **The shares:**
   153	     - the share of **decided** figure-question answers (supported or unsupported) that are unsupported;
   154	     - the same for yes/no questions;
   155	     - the share of decided worth marks that are trivia or duplicate.
   156	   - **Reported beside, not in the shares:** insufficient and no answer, with the number of splits and how each counting resolved them.
   157	   - **The bar:** the new system's share may not be more than 10 points above the old system's.
   158	   - **Exercised:** each subgroup needs at least 10 cards per system decided without a split (item 7), or it is "not exercised".
   159	9. **The controls,** outside every product count:
   160	   - 6 planted wrong answers (a rung moved, 3 per system). Each needs an explicit unsupported or insufficient mark, or the reading is invalid.
   161	   - 4 supported controls, confirmed first by a separate reader.
   162	   - 3 hidden repeats.
   163	10. **No release verdict without six complete draws.** A draw that cannot finish (stopped after D449's one redo, or by its guard) gives no
   164	    release verdict. What finished goes to Alex as a diagnostic, with the cause.
   165	    - No replacement draw counts toward the verdict. D431's "same day" means both systems' sends fall on one calendar day
   166	      (`WIRING-DESIGN.md`).
   167	    - The reviewers read that differently (Codex: the send day; the Claude reviewer: the window). The council took the cheaper, reversible
   168	      course (D439): a later replacement would be a diagnostic only.
   169	11. **The verdicts, tested in this order:**
   170	    1. **Invalid:** a plant without an explicit rejection, fewer than 3 of 4 supported controls accepted, or fewer than 2 of 3 repeats marked
   171	       the same.
   172	    2. **Too small:** N < 20, or fewer than 30 distinct questions in any system-draw.
   173	    3. **Practical lead:** a − b ≥ max(3, ⌈0.15·N⌉) and a ≥ 2b under both countings, with every guardrail exercised and passing under both.
   174	    4. **Practical loss:** the mirror under both countings, or a breach in an exercised guardrail under both.
   175	    5. **No practical winner:** everything else.
