   102	### 1.4 What the new system does differently
   103	
   104	| Choice | Running pipeline | New system | Shown so far |
   105	|---|---|---|---|
   106	| What reading an item sees | Open questions with filing guidance, from the start of the day. | Every open thesis as the previous item left it, and never the evidence history (section 4). | The request no longer grows with the evidence. |
   107	| What opens a question | Extraction proposes candidates with answer choices; a matcher and a collision judge decide. | Step 1 decides in the same reading, and only for a big matter; a company's own figures are conditions, not questions (D382, D384). | 1 of 10 opened wrongly on 50 fresh articles (D386). Opening is also where it loses most against the ruler (section 8). |
   108	| A useful fact no question takes | Thrown away. | Kept without links, and offered to each new question when it opens (D383). | 0 thrown away against 278 in one fresh week; but 389 of 569 kept statements were read by no judgement that week (section 10). |
   109	| Finding the earlier version of a statement | Shared words, inside one question. | Semantic search across the company, then Jev compares (section 5). | 141 of 143 earlier versions found; 1 clear wrong merge in 57 (D386). |
   110	| The same statement reported again | A separate record. | One group; each report keeps its own text and source (section 6). | Later details are kept (22 = 22, a storage check). Copies are still 31–34% of what the judge reads, against 26–31% in the running pipeline. |
   111	| Answer choices | Written by extraction with the candidate. | A separate step at opening: an event has three outcomes; a figure has five ranges against a reference with the favourable end registered; the due day follows rules (section 7). | Its quality gate is not met (section 8). |
   112	| Judging and closing | The frozen judge, daily; closing on the due day. | The same judge and the same closing, unchanged (D354, D567). | By design, differences come only from what reaches the judge. |
   113	
   114	On the frozen extraction gold, Step 1 keeps more of what matters: helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234 in three draws, against 167, 171 and
   115	162 for the running extraction (core 85–86 of 86 against 75–76). The running pipeline's draws were stored runs from another day, so
   116	this is a lead, not a same-day comparison.
   117	
   118	**What is not shown yet.** That the new system gives better answers end to end. Every comparison so far is a diagnostic: the seeded
   119	test was inconclusive (18, 18, 17 against 17, 15, 16 of 23); on one fresh week the difference was "not clear" by the rule written
   120	beforehand (D489). The weakest point is named (D559): the system often cannot turn an important matter into a question it can answer
   121	and follow. It also costs more: about $3 a draw for the whole new system (Step 1 about $2.5 of it) against about $0.45 for the running
   122	pipeline, and about 2 hours 20 minutes a draw because items are read one after another (an estimate,
   123	`e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/SLICE-RESULT.md`). Correct comes
   124	before cheap; cost is recorded as a baseline.
   125	
   126	### 1.5 What decides that it replaces the running pipeline (D665, superseding D548's gate order)
   127	
   128	Alex asked the council to determine whether the six lines still make sense and what decides readiness (D664: "does it make sense now run your
   129	council to determine"). The council's consensus is D665.
   130	
   131	**The release decision is one product comparison against the running pipeline.**
   132	- **The systems.** poc-v118 is replayed against the new system as configured and hashed (astra-frozen; v5.5 excluded). Both start from empty
   133	  over 2026-07-01 to 07-19, three draws each, and are scored on the unread weeks from 07-20 up to 09-20, as far as the full exposure census
   134	  supports.
   135	- **The main quantity: big matters served.**
   136	  - Both reader families list the scored weeks' big matters from the articles, blind to any output, and freeze the list.
   137	  - A system serves a matter when, in at least two of three draws, it holds a question both families mark worth tracking and whose latest
   138	    answer both mark supported. The cards are read by the council (D663), with Alex spot-checking splits.
   139	  - With a = matters served only by the new system, b = only by the old, and N = all listed matters, written before the first send and computed
   140	    under both split countings:
   141	    - **Practical lead:** a − b ≥ max(3, 15% of N) and a ≥ 2b. Every guardrail subgroup must meet its frozen minimum and pass under both
   142	      countings.
   143	    - **Practical loss:** the mirror of the lead, or a guardrail breach confirmed under both countings.
   144	    - **No practical winner:** everything else, including a verdict a split could change, or a guardrail that was not exercised.
   145	    - **Too small:** fewer than 20 distinct matters, or fewer than 30 distinct questions per system per draw.
   146	    - **Invalid:** a planted wrong answer accepted, fewer than 3 of 4 supported controls accepted, or fewer than 2 of 3 repeats marked the same.
   147	- **The guardrails:** the unsupported share for figures, the unsupported share for yes/no questions, and the not-worth-tracking share, none
   148	  more than 10 points above the old system's. D428's original limits are printed beside them.
   149	- **Printed beside the verdict, not deciding:** a sign test and an interval (illustrative unless the frozen grouping supports them); the
   150	  frozen-reference reading of 07-01 to 07-14 (D431, superseded on this point only); and the proposed absolute floors.
   151	- **Kept from the earlier gates:**
   152	  - **The six lines of D400** are measured and reported on every ladder run. They no longer block release.
   153	  - **The parts code decides stay hard checks:** figure ranges meet end to end; the favourable end is the same across sends; the due day is
   154	    consistent in at least nine of ten.
   155	  - **Step 2's three checks stay,** as D427 and D431 set them.
   156	  - **Stage B's control line waits** with D658.
   157	- **Back to Alex,** before any release engineering resumes:
   158	  - a practical lead, with the split cards for his spot check;
   159	  - a practical loss, at once (D544);
   160	  - no practical winner, too small, or invalid, with the counts;
   161	  - no verdict by 2026-12-01.
   162	
   163	| Earlier gate | Now |

