   294	## 5. Step 2: find and compare related evidence
   295	
   296	### Why this is separate
   297	
   298	An open thesis may accumulate many evidence records. Step 1 needs to understand the article and the open research; it does not need to read every prior statement to do that. Step 2 uses each proposed `{thesis, evidence}` association to find the history worth comparing.
   299	
   300	### Find candidate evidence
   301	
   302	Semantic search runs over the evidence library. It uses the thesis and incoming evidence to find top-N related evidence across the current entity, including evidence linked to other theses. The thesis is search context, not a restriction to evidence already linked to that thesis. This also applies when the proposed thesis is new. For example, “raised its revenue outlook” and “expects higher sales” can be related despite different wording. Alex chose this entity-wide scope on 2026-11-06.
   303	
   304	Search returns **candidates**, not a final decision. As built and frozen (`step2/compare.py`; D381, D427): one query per association (the thesis question, then the incoming summary and passages), plus one query of the record's own text when it has no association or lists the company's figures; each query ranks the original record of every stored group by cosine similarity of `text-embedding-3-small` embeddings and keeps the top 10; the union is compared. Figures never enter a search or comparison text. Measured: the earlier version of a repeat was among the candidates for 112 of 113 known pairs in the first forward run and 141 of 143 at the acceptance on fresh articles (D386). Speed and cost under production load are not measured.
   305	
   306	A match outside the returned set will not be considered by the comparison. Search quality therefore matters independently from the model's ability to compare the records it receives.
   307	
   308	### Compare with Jev
   309	
   310	**As built and frozen (2026-11-08, accepted D381, frozen D427; [round log](step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§24, 31–35):**
   311	- Comparison reads the full passages; the 600-character cut is removed.
   312	- An unlinked record also searches by its own text.
   313	- `figures` never enter a search or comparison text.
   314	- Jev is asked the clean path's own piece question, aligned with line 2's test: a repeat reports the stored piece's main statement, and
   315	  an official's denial of a report is a revision.
   316	- When a thesis opens, every stored unlinked group from earlier articles is judged against it in batches of ten (a yes/no question; the
   317	  company's overall results inform only a revenue or earnings thesis). Each judged pair is recorded once.
   318	- On c6's records: search 97.9%, wrong merges 1.9% (2 of 104), both lines met; line 3 not re-measured. At the acceptance on 50 fresh
   319	  articles (D386, one draw): search 141 of 143, clear wrong merges 1 of 57 plus 2 overlaps.
   320	- Known weakness, recorded and not tuned (D423): "later version" relations. In one saved end-to-end draw, two source readers found 9
   321	  of 30 such relations join two different statements, most of them NVIDIA's answer to a report linked to a neighbouring statement of
   322	  the same story; a wording change failed its line (Step 2 log §14).
   323	
   324	Jev compares the new evidence with the returned evidence. The intended outcomes are:
   325	
   326	| Outcome | Meaning | Example |
   327	|---|---|---|
   328	| Repeat | The new material presents the same statement again. | Another article reports the same company forecast. |
   329	| Revision | A later account changes the same statement. | The company revises its forecast from 12% to 25%. |
   330	| New evidence | It is not identified as a repeat or revision of the evidence compared. | A different analyst offers a separate forecast. |
   331	
   332	The words, amount, speaker, source and time can matter to the comparison. A later statement about the same subject is not necessarily a revision. A matching amount alone does not prove that two statements are the same.
   333	
   334	The result “new evidence” is the outcome of the comparison performed; it is not proof that no matching evidence exists elsewhere in the library. When the incoming evidence is judged a repeat of several candidates, it joins only the group Jev judges it most like: the highest same-statement probability, with ties going to the earliest group (Alex, 2026-11-08). A comparison that fails is not saved and is reported.
   335	
   336	**Repeat handoff:** when Jev identifies a repeat, the comparison must retain the identity of the existing evidence record it was compared against. Step 3 uses that identity to place the incoming record in the existing record's group, adding its source and any missing thesis link. The typed Jev response need not generate the record ID: the caller can associate the choice with the candidate it supplied. The rule for several matching candidates is stated above.
   337	
   338	**Step 2 acceptance (Alex, 2026-11-08).** Three lines apply:
   339	1. For incoming evidence whose earlier version is in the library, search returns it at least 90% of the time.
   340	2. At most 2% of merges are clearly wrong.
   341	3. Grouped dossiers keep no fewer details added by later reports than no merging does.
   342	
   343	The first measurement against these lines is in the [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12; the isolated modules are `step2/compare.py` and `step3/library.py`.
   344	
   345	**Frozen (D427, Alex, 2026-11-11: "好jev 收尾冻结").** Step 2 and Step 3's grouping library stay as accepted and measured (Step 2 log §§12–18); `tests/test_frozen_clean_step2.py` fails the suite when one of their files moves. They go to production only with the whole clean path, after Step 4 meets its lines, through an end-to-end comparison against production on the frozen ruler (three draws each), with these three lines checked on that run. A change is a new version that only Alex opens; while he is not active, the council may open it (D439), never the frozen ruler. Five open defects are carried with the freeze (Step 2 log §17).
   346	
   347	The diagram's intended sequence is: use `{thesis, incoming evidence}` to search the evidence library; pass the returned candidates and incoming evidence to Jev; pass the comparison result and the proposed association to Step 3. For a new thesis, search the same entity-wide evidence library. The frozen Jev request appears in [section 13](#13-step-2-prompt-jev-request-frozen).
   348	
   349	## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links
   350	
   351	Step 3 takes the proposals from Step 1 and the comparison outcomes from Step 2 and writes the resulting research information.
   352	
     7	a type test, a patched attribute) are settled in code and its $0 tests, which the next review reads instead of prose. **Purpose:** run the clean path end to end on fresh articles (Step 1 c8 → frozen Steps 2–3 → the ladder step as it ran in the
     8	fourth Stage A → the frozen judge) and, the same day, production v118, as a diagnostic under D442; never D427's acceptance while Step 4's
     9	lines are unmet. Nothing frozen changes: the ruler, Steps 2–3 (D427), the ladder step's configuration (D426), the judge. "Same day" means
    10	the model sends of both systems on one calendar day; the article window (v108's 243 items, 2026-07-01..07-14) is a fixed input.
